    "content": "# TRANSMISSION XIV — The Compost\n**Degree 13 · Key: Death · Degree name: The Compost**\n**Shadow:** clinging\n**Práctica:** bury one dead thing and let it feed the next\n\n> **PRIVATE DRAFT — NOT LIVE CANON.** `release=false`. Do not copy into `NORTHSTAR_PRIME`, publish, deploy, or charge.\n> Fictional NorthStar Mystery School work. Symbolic shadow-work only. **Not therapy, medicine, legal advice, or a real-world initiatory authority.** VORATH is original fiction, not a god or a lineage.\n> Structure note (2026-09-18 PT): live transmissions 01–05 were not re-opened this pass. Headings follow the private drafts VI–VIII and the recovered Path table. Align before any future canon merge. This file is a draft, not a rite you can join.\n\n**After the Suspension:** one outcome is no longer eating the evening. Death, in this Path, is not a body and not a command. The degree name is Compost. The shadow is clinging: watering what is already finished because the pot was expensive and the story was that you keep things alive.\n\n## 1. Street fact\n\nThe basil on the sill is brown. You water it anyway. The soil smells like a closet. On the same sill sits a new pot, unopened, because opening it would admit the old one is done. Next to both, a folder on the laptop still titled FINAL, about a project, a role, or a launch date that has passed. You click it the way you water the plant: a little, often, never enough to move it. The window is ordinary. The street below is buying bread. You are keeping office hours for a ghost that is a plan, not a person.\n\n## 2. Interior pressure\n\nClinging is not love and not grief's whole shape. If what you cannot set down is a person who died, or a loss that still takes the air out of a room, this práctica is the wrong size. Grief is not a chore the Path assigns. Put the fiction down. Use real support if you need it. The Dark Mirror is not a bereavement service and not a doctor.\n\nWhat this degree can hold is smaller and ruder: a dead role, a dead draft, a dead self-portrait (\"the one who stays late,\" \"the one this city was for\"). You keep it on the sill because burial feels like insult, and because the next living thing would require a decision. Degree 12 released a grip on an outcome that might still arrive. This degree meets a thing that will not. Pretending otherwise is how FINAL stays open and the new pot stays shut.\n\nDo not mysticize rot. A dead plant is a dead plant. It does not mean you are dying, cursed, or being initiated by decay. The Death key is a structural label for change already in the Path table. It is not an omen and not a rite from a living funeral tradition. This draft will not borrow those rites. Schizocandy glitter on a brown leaf is color, not a message, not a diagnosis, not a villain.\n\n## 3. Symbolic architecture\n\nBehind the food court, a service courtyard holds bins marked SCRAPS and a cracked planter. No tombs. No costumes of mourning. The Alien Choir tries to file the basil under ETERNAL. Cross that out. MOTE is a small worm in clean soil, unglamorous, doing a job. The Loomwheel, if it turns beyond the wall, eats loops. A plant watered after it is dead is a loop. So is a folder renamed FINAL_FINAL.\n\nVORATH's prism splits \"this mattered\" from \"this must remain on the sill.\" Both can be true in sequence, not at once. The Eye witnesses whether you bury the thing. It does not watch the dead, and it does not inventory a real family's loss. Bones, if the dream offers them, are a wrong turn. Leave. This fiction's holdings are invented, and this transmission uses a plant and a folder on purpose.\n\n**Paired shadow — Shrine.** The dead thing stays visible as identity. You tell the story of the basil, the job, the version of you, until guests learn to admire the brown leaves. Clinging calls itself honoring. Honoring would have let the nutrients move.\n\n**Paired shadow — Erase.** You throw out the pot, the folder, and the lesson in one bag, then start the same project under a new name next month. Nothing is fed forward. The sill looks clean. The pattern is intact, which is clinging with better housekeeping.\n\nNeither is a moral verdict on how anyone mourns a person. This page is about plans and roles. It does not diagnose depression, and it does not tell you to \"move on\" from a human being.\n\n## 4. Moral choice\n\nName one dead thing that is not a person. You already know it is dead because you have been performing life support. Say the death in one line, without eulogy: \"The launch date was June. It is September.\" \"I do not work there.\" \"The basil is done.\"\n\nThen bury it so something living can use the room.\n\n- **Bin and box.** The plant goes to the food-scrap bin or into soil, not the windowsill. The folder moves to a DONE box, off the desktop. On a scrap, one line: what it fed, a skill or a fact, not a halo. Cost: you are no longer the person whose sill proves effort. The effort already happened. It does not need a corpse to stay true.\n- **Then one living next, small.** Open the new pot and set it where the old one was, or name one hour next week for the next piece of work, not both if both would scatter you. The Drive's rule still holds: one force. Cost: the next thing is embarrassingly alive and unfinished. That is the point of compost. It feeds a start, not a monument.\n\nWhat you will not do: raid a real grave, a real culture's funeral, or someone else's mourning as your aesthetic. Do not destroy another person's copy of a shared thing without their say. Do not call a living relationship dead so you can avoid a hard conversation. That is Erase. If it is alive and painful, it belongs to an earlier degree, not to a bin.\n\n## 5. Material return\n\nDo the burial today, in miniature. Hands in soil or the bin, then soap. The new pot watered once, or the hour written on a calendar you use. Clear the sill so the window is a window. *All is remembering* — remember the one line of what it fed, then let the object go. The ceremony is the making of an empty square of light, not a speech at a ceremony you do not own.\n\n## The práctica\n\nBury one dead thing and let it feed the next.\n\nPrivate page:\n\n1. The dead thing, a plan or a role, not a person. If you wrote a person's name, stop. This is the wrong sheet.\n2. How you have been watering it.\n3. The one nutrient worth keeping, a sentence.\n4. Where it went: bin, box, shelf cleared.\n5. The next living thing, one, and when it gets its first small care.\n\nOne burial. A purge of the whole apartment is Erase.\n\n## Video — Raw Feed cut (about 75 seconds)\n\n**Picture:** brown basil, a watering can used anyway, an unopened pot, a folder titled FINAL. Cut to a service-courtyard scrap bin, no graves, a worm in soil, MOTE barely more than that. Last shot: empty sill, new pot, hands washed, a calendar hour circled. No body. No funeral costume. No sacred rite. No gothic glamour.\n\n**Spoken:**\n\nI was watering a dead plant and opening a folder whose date had passed. I called that keeping faith. It was clinging. The new pot stayed shut so I would not have to admit the sill was finished.\n\nDeath, in this story, is compost. Not a person. Not an omen. Two cheap exits: make a shrine of the brown leaves, or throw out the lesson and regrow the same mistake under a new name.\n\nThe practice is one dead plan. I bury it in a bin or a box. I keep one sentence of what it taught. I give the next living thing one hour. Then I wash my hands.\n\nThe reveler is you. What stares back is not the end. It is the empty square of light, and love, if that word belongs here, is letting the dead thing feed something that can still grow.\n\n**On screen:** Fiction. Not grief counseling. Not medical advice. Not a funeral rite.\n\n## What this transmission is not\n\nIt does not claim the Death key as necromancy, prophecy, or a reconstruction of any culture's care for the dead. If your dead thing is a someone, this transmission steps aside. Compost is for finished plans. People are not plans.\n"
